      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what that means and why it is so important:\n\n**1. The Ingredients**\nWater is made up of two simple elements:\n*   **Hydrogen:** A gas that is extremely flammable.\n*   **Oxygen:** A gas that we need to breathe to survive.\n\nWhen two hydrogen atoms bond with one oxygen atom, they create a water molecule.\n\n**2. Its Unique Structure**\nWater is a **polar molecule**. This means the molecule has a slightly positive end and a slightly negative end. This polarity is what makes water special:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is the main transport system in the human body (carrying nutrients to cells) and in nature (carrying nutrients through soil).\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together tightly at the surface, which is why water forms droplets and why some insects can walk on water.\n\n**3. The Three States of Matter**\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, the molecules move slowly and lock into a rigid crystal structure.\n*   **Liquid:** At room temperature, the molecules move freely but stay close together.\n*   **Gas (Steam):** When heated, the molecules move so fast that they fly apart into the air.\n\n**4. Why It Is Essential for Life**\nWater is the foundation of life on Earth. It is the primary component of all living things (humans are about 60% water). It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, protects the spinal cord and other sensitive tissues, and helps remove waste."
